  to care. So choosing fields now is implicitly choosing what sub-project 3 can notify about.

  Two fields are non-negotiable — a title and a status — and neither is interesting on its own. The ones that carry
  notification weight:

  - [1mAssignee[0m — the single strongest "cares about" signal, and the one that makes reassignment a notifiable event.
    Without it, "tasks they care about" has almost nothing to key on.
  - [1mDue date[0m — the only field that changes [3mwithout anyone touching it[0m, which means time-based notifications (due soon,
    overdue) and therefore a scheduler. That's real infrastructure, so it's worth deciding deliberately rather than
    inheriting.
  - [1mComments[0m — turn tasks into threads and create participant-based interest ("I commented, so I care"). The largest
    addition: a second entity, its own endpoints, its own event type.
  - [1mDescription[0m — cheap, but noisy to notify on. Usually worth storing and [3mnot[0m notifying about.
  - [1mPriority / labels[0m — cheap to add, weak notification value in v1.

  I'd recommend [1mtitle, status, assignee, due date, description[0m for v1, holding comments back. That gives sub-project 3
  three genuinely useful event types (assigned, status changed, due soon) without pulling a threaded-discussion
  subsystem into the task layer. Comments are additive later — they don't invalidate the schema.

  Due date is the one I'd flag: including it commits you to a scheduled job runner, not just request-triggered events.

